Library Management System – Persistence Update

Date: February 10, 2026

Summary
Persistent storage was added so data is saved between program runs. JSON files are now used to store patrons, books, and loans. This document was added to give the team a clear overview of what changed and how it works.

What now persists
Patrons are stored in patrons.json
Books are stored in books.json
Loans are stored in loans.json

Application behavior
When the program starts, each collection automatically attempts to load its JSON file.
When the program exits, all current data is automatically saved.
If the JSON files do not already exist, the program still runs normally and creates them on exit.

Code changes by file

BooksCollection.h
Added load and save function declarations for JSON files.
Added a file path string to track the data file.

BooksCollection.cpp
Implemented JSON serialization and deserialization.
The constructor sets the data file path to books.json and loads saved data.
The destructor saves the current data before cleanup.
The next book ID is restored based on the highest loaded book ID to prevent duplicates.

PatronsCollection.h
Added a constructor declaration.
Added load and save function declarations for JSON files.
Added a file path string for patron data.

PatronsCollection.cpp
Implemented JSON serialization and deserialization.
The constructor loads data from patrons.json.
The destructor saves data on shutdown.
Persisted fields include patron ID, name, fine balance, and number of checked-out books.

Loans.h
Added static methods to get and set the next loan ID.
This allows loan numbering to continue correctly after loading saved data.

Loans.cpp
Implemented the new static loan ID setter and getter.

LoansCollection.h
Added constructor and destructor declarations.
Added load and save function declarations for JSON files.
Added a file path string for loan data.

LoansCollection.cpp
Implemented JSON serialization and deserialization.
The constructor loads data from loans.json.
The destructor saves loan data on shutdown.
Stored fields include loan ID, book ID, patron ID, due date, and loan status.
After loading, the next loan ID is set to the highest loaded ID plus one to avoid collisions.

main.cpp
Added a startup message indicating saved data is loaded if available.
Added a shutdown message indicating data is being saved.
No changes were made to menu behavior or user interaction.

Validation performed
A syntax-only compile was run using g++ with C++17 enabled.
No compilation errors were reported.

Quick test steps
Run the application and add patrons, books, and at least one loan.
Exit the application.
Verify that patrons.json, books.json, and loans.json were created and contain data.
Run the application again and confirm that the previously entered records are still available.

Notes
The JSON parser is intentionally simple and expects the same structure the program writes.
Manually changing the JSON structure may cause records not to load correctly.
